# This box has core.autocrlf=true. Left alone, a fresh checkout would give the
# shell scripts CRLF endings — and they are deployed byte-for-byte to the Pi,
# where /bin/dash then chokes on the trailing \r. Pin them to LF.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.conf    text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.snippet text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf

# The release driver runs on Windows, so CRLF is the native form. It is also
# kept strictly ASCII: PowerShell 5.1 reads .ps1 as ANSI, where an em-dash
# decodes to a smart quote that silently terminates a string mid-script.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# The opposite case: the FAT-partition README is read in Windows Notepad by
# people who have never seen a Pi. It must keep CRLF or it renders as one
# run-on block. `-text` = never normalise, in either direction.
dist/fat-x16-README.TXT -text
